{"results":[{"url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/archive/home","final_url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/archive/home","title":"World Bank Group Archives","description":null,"language":"en","text":"# World Bank Group Archives\n\nThe Archives preserves the institutional memory of the World Bank Group and provides public access to records of the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) and the International Development Association (IDA).\n\nAbout Us\n\n* Discovery Tool\n\n  To mark the 80th anniversary of the first Annual Meetings in September 1946, the Archives has created a timeline highlighting the 28 Annual Meetings held in member countries outside Washington, DC. featuring archival records, photos, a/v, and more!\n* Exhibit\n\n  To mark the thirtieth anniversary of James D. Wolfensohn’s arrival as president of the World Bank Group, the Archives launched a series of product releases and events, including an exhibit at our headquarters in Washington, DC. Explore a virtual reality version of the exhibit featuring archival records, audiovisual materials, and more!\n* Finding Aids\n\n  ## New descriptions published on Archives Catalog\n\n  The World Bank Group Archives regularly publishes new series-level archival descriptions on its Archives Catalog. These descriptions inform Access to Information requests by external researchers and facilitate access to digitized records. Check out our newest releases, including the urban development sector and two former managing directors.\n* The WBG Archives is launching a series of product releases and events to celebrate President Wolfensohn’s legacy and to draw attention and facilitate access to records from his presidency.\n* Finding Aids\n\n  ## Newly Updated Inventory Lists in Online Exhibits\n\n  The Archives regularly publishes online exhibits on topics from the institution’s history. Inventory lists containing relevant archival records and links to digitized records are included for some of these exhibits. The Archives has recently updated the lists for these exhibits.\n* Exhibit\n\n  In recognition of Black History Month, this World Bank Group Archives exhibit illuminates what we have learned from the documentary evidence in our holdings, and presents the inspiring people, programs, and perspectives related to the WBG's ongoing commitment to diversity, equity and inclusion.\n\n## World Bank Group Historical Timeline\n\nThe timeline offers an engaging way to learn about the important moments in the history of the World Bank while discovering the gems of the World Bank Group Archives.\n\n## Flagship Discovery Tools\n\nThe Archives Catalog contains archival descriptions and provides access to digitized records.\n\nThe Multimedia Catalog provides access to thousands of photos and recordings documenting World Bank operations, presidents, official signings, and more.\n\nThe Oral History Program website contains transcripts, summaries, and audio clips of interviews with former World Bank staff.\n\nTrack World Bank membership over time and access historical profiles for every member country.\n\n## Archives Spotlight\n\nExhibit\n\nOn July 1, 1944, delegates from forty-four nations met in Bretton Woods, New Hampshire to agree on a new system of international cooperation. Learn more about this historic meeting and view archival records, audiovisual materials, and more.\n\nwebsite\n\nThe Web Archives provides access to World Bank websites that have been discontinued or significantly updated. Archived sites date from 1998 to the present.\n\nVideo\n\nThis short promotional video describes the possibilities available to researchers when they “jump in and explore” the holdings of the World Bank Group Archives.\n\nWebsite\n\nAccess Bank Group-authored reports, including Board Documents, Economic and Sector Work reports, project documents, and publications and research.\n\nWebsite\n\nContains country director data from 1972 to 2025.\n\nWebsite\n\nProvides contextual information for each World Bank project, past and present, as well as a folder list of archival records that relate to each project.\n\nresource\n\nThe Roadmap is a multi-part toolkit, designed to help governments and public-sector organizations implement improvements in records management.\n\nExternal Partnership\n\nSee the results when machine learning strategies are applied to Robert S. McNamara’s World Bank records.\n\nPublication\n\nThis accessible introduction to conducting research in the Archives consolidates all the information you need to begin your research.\n\n## Connect\n\nAccess to WBG records is governed by the Access to Information Policy.\n\nLearn More\n\n### Submit a Request\n\nAfter researching what materials you need in our catalog, submit a request for access.\n\nLearn More\n\nThe Archives' innovative Digital Preservation Program paves the way for the future.\n\nGo Inside\n\nKeep up with the latest news and releases from the WBG Archives.\n\nFollow Us on Twitter","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":455.56650500020623,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}